WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.000
Because I usually take the role of trying to explain to people how wonderful the new

00:22.200 --> 00:27.520
technologies that are coming along are going to be. And I thought that since I was among

00:27.520 --> 00:34.520
friends here, I would tell you what I really think. And try to look back and try to understand

00:35.000 --> 00:42.000
what is really going on here with these amazing jumps in technology. It seems so fast that

00:43.960 --> 00:48.760
we can barely keep on top of it. So I'm going to start out by showing just one very boring

00:48.760 --> 00:55.760
technology slide. And then, so if you can just turn on the slide that's on. This is

00:55.760 --> 01:02.720
just a random slide that I picked out of my file. And what I want to show you is not so

01:02.720 --> 01:06.680
much the details of this slide, but the general form of it. This happens to be a slide of

01:06.680 --> 01:12.560
some analysis that we were doing about the power of risk microprocessors versus the power

01:12.560 --> 01:19.480
of local area networks. And the interesting thing about it is that this slide, like so

01:19.480 --> 01:25.000
many technology slides that we're used to, is a sort of a straight line on a semi-log

01:25.000 --> 01:32.000
curve. In other words, every step here represents an order of magnitude and performance gain.

01:32.160 --> 01:36.920
And we're sort of the, this is a new thing that we talk about technology on semi-log

01:36.920 --> 01:41.440
curve. Something really weird is going on here. And that's basically what I'm going

01:41.440 --> 01:48.440
to be talking about. So if you bring up the lights. If you could bring up the lights higher

01:49.680 --> 01:54.960
because I'm just going to use a piece of paper here. Now, why do we draw technology curves

01:54.960 --> 02:00.480
on semi-log curves? Well, the answer is if I drew it on a normal curve, where let's say

02:00.480 --> 02:07.000
this is years, this is time of some sort, and this is whatever measure of the technology

02:07.000 --> 02:14.000
that I'm trying to graph, the graphs look sort of silly. They sort of go like this.

02:14.840 --> 02:21.840
And they don't tell us much. Now, if I graph, for instance, some other technology, say transportation

02:23.400 --> 02:27.680
technology, on a semi-log curve, it would look very stupid. It would look like a flat

02:27.680 --> 02:33.280
line. But when something like this happens, things are qualitatively changing. So if transportation

02:33.280 --> 02:39.680
technology was moving along as fast as microprocessor technology, then sort of day after tomorrow

02:39.760 --> 02:45.560
I would be able to get in a taxi cab and be in Tokyo in 30 seconds. It's not moving like

02:45.560 --> 02:50.040
that. And there's nothing precedented in the history of technology development of this

02:50.040 --> 02:55.640
kind of self-feeding growth where you go by orders of magnitude every few years. Now,

02:55.640 --> 03:01.920
the question that I'd like to ask is if you look at these exponential curves, they don't

03:01.920 --> 03:08.920
go on forever. Things just can't possibly keep changing as fast as they are. One of

03:09.240 --> 03:15.520
two things is going to happen. Either it's going to turn into sort of classical S-curve

03:15.520 --> 03:22.520
like this until something totally different comes along. Or maybe it's going to do this.

03:23.000 --> 03:27.800
That's about all it can do. Now, I'm an optimist, so I sort of think it's probably going to

03:27.800 --> 03:32.900
do something like that. If so, that means that what we're in the middle of right now

03:32.900 --> 03:37.600
is a transition. We're sort of on this line in a transition from the way that the world

03:37.600 --> 03:42.880
used to be to some new way that the world is. And so what I'm trying to ask, and I've

03:42.880 --> 03:47.840
been asking myself, is what's this new way that the world is? What's the new state that

03:47.840 --> 03:53.160
the world is heading toward? Because the transition seems very, very confusing when we're right

03:53.160 --> 04:00.040
in the middle of it. Now, when I was a kid growing up, the future was kind of the year

04:00.040 --> 04:07.040
2000. And people used to talk about what would happen in the year 2000. Now, here's a conference

04:07.200 --> 04:11.040
in which people talk about the future. And you notice that the future is still at about

04:11.040 --> 04:15.400
the year 2000. That's about as far as we go out. So, in other words, the future has kind

04:15.400 --> 04:22.400
of been shrinking one year per year for my whole lifetime. Now, I think that the reason

04:24.760 --> 04:30.040
is, is because we all feel that something's happening there. That transition is happening.

04:30.040 --> 04:34.680
We can all sense it. And we know that it just doesn't make too much sense to think out 30,

04:34.680 --> 04:39.280
30, 50 years. Because everything's going to be so different that a simple extrapolation

04:39.280 --> 04:44.280
of what we're doing just doesn't make any sense at all. So, what I would like to talk

04:44.280 --> 04:50.480
about is what that could be, what that transition could be that we're going through. Now, in

04:50.480 --> 04:55.840
order to do that, I'm going to have to talk about a bunch of stuff that really has nothing

04:55.840 --> 05:00.280
to do with technology and computers. Because I think the only way to understand this is

05:00.320 --> 05:06.440
to really step back and take a long time scale look at things. So, the time scale that I

05:06.440 --> 05:13.440
would like to look at this on is the time scale of life on Earth. So, I think this picture

05:15.480 --> 05:21.440
makes sense if you look at it a few billion years at a time. So, if you go back about

05:21.440 --> 05:27.000
two and a half billion years, the Earth was this big, sterile hunk of rock with a lot

05:27.040 --> 05:33.080
of chemicals floating around on it. And if you look at the way that the chemicals got

05:33.080 --> 05:39.360
organized, we begin to get a pretty good idea of how they do it. And I think that there's

05:39.360 --> 05:42.440
theories that are beginning to understand about how it started with RNA. But I'm going

05:42.440 --> 05:48.060
to tell a sort of simple story of it, which is that at that time there were little drops

05:48.060 --> 05:53.560
of oil floating around with all kinds of different recipes of chemicals in them. And some of

05:53.600 --> 05:58.400
those drops of oils had a particular combination of chemicals in them, which caused them to

05:58.400 --> 06:03.640
incorporate chemicals from the outside and grow the drops of oil. And those that were

06:03.640 --> 06:09.480
like that started to split and divide. And those were the most primitive forms of cells

06:09.480 --> 06:14.600
in a sense, those little drops of oil. But now, those drops of oil weren't really life

06:14.600 --> 06:19.120
as we say it now because every one of them was a little random recipe of chemicals. And

06:19.120 --> 06:24.920
sometime it divided something different. I mean, it got sort of unequal division of

06:24.920 --> 06:30.000
the chemicals within it. And so every drop was a little bit different. In fact, the drops

06:30.000 --> 06:33.920
that were different in a way that caused them to be better at incorporating chemicals around

06:33.920 --> 06:39.880
them grew more, incorporated more chemicals, and divided more. So those tended to live

06:39.880 --> 06:46.880
longer, get expressed more. Now, that's sort of just a very simple chemical form of life.

06:47.880 --> 06:54.880
But when things got interesting was when these drops learned a trick of abstraction. Somehow,

06:57.240 --> 07:02.120
by ways that we don't quite understand, these little drops learned to write down information.

07:02.120 --> 07:07.760
They learned to record the information that was the recipe of the cell onto a particular

07:07.760 --> 07:14.080
kind of chemical called DNA. So in other words, they worked out in this sort of mindless evolutionary

07:14.080 --> 07:19.600
way a form of writing that let them write down what they were so that that way of writing

07:19.600 --> 07:24.680
it down could get copied. The amazing thing is that that way of writing seems to have

07:24.680 --> 07:29.320
stayed steady since it evolved two and a half billion years ago. In fact, the recipe for

07:29.320 --> 07:34.680
us, our genes is exactly that same code in that same way of writing. In fact, every living

07:34.680 --> 07:40.000
creature is sort of written in exactly the same set of letters in the same code. In fact,

07:40.000 --> 07:44.040
one of the things that I did just for amusement purposes is we can now write things in this

07:44.040 --> 07:51.040
code. And I've got here a little 100 micrograms of white powder, which I try not to let the

07:53.000 --> 08:00.000
security people see at airports. But this has in it, what I did is I took this code.

08:00.760 --> 08:05.000
The code has standard letters that we use for symbolizing it. And I wrote my business

08:05.000 --> 08:10.880
card onto a piece of DNA and amplified it 10 to the 22 times. So if anyone would like

08:10.880 --> 08:14.800
100 million copies of my business card, I have money for everyone in the room, and in

08:14.800 --> 08:21.800
fact everyone in the world. And it's right here. So if I had really been an egotist,

08:28.480 --> 08:35.480
I would have put it into a virus and released it in the room. So what was the next step?

08:41.520 --> 08:45.920
Writing down the DNA was an interesting step. And that caused these cells to, that kept

08:45.920 --> 08:49.920
them happy for another billion years. But then there was another really interesting

08:49.920 --> 08:54.540
step where things became completely different, which is these cells started exchanging and

08:54.540 --> 08:58.840
communicating information so that they began to get communities of cells. I don't know

08:58.840 --> 09:04.400
if you know this, but bacteria can actually exchange DNA. That's why, for instance, antibiotic

09:04.400 --> 09:09.400
resistance has evolved. Some bacteria figured out how to stay away from psilon, and it went

09:09.440 --> 09:14.680
around sort of trading its little DNA information with other bacteria, and now we have a lot

09:14.680 --> 09:20.480
of bacteria that are resistant to psilon because bacteria communicate. Now what this communication

09:20.480 --> 09:25.520
allowed was communities to form that in some sense were in the same boat together. They

09:25.520 --> 09:31.800
were synergistic. So they survived or they failed together, which means that if a community

09:31.800 --> 09:37.760
was very successful, all the individuals in that community were repeated more, and they

09:37.800 --> 09:43.320
were favored by evolution. Now the transition point happened when these communities got

09:43.320 --> 09:48.320
so close that in fact they got together and decided to write down the whole recipe for

09:48.320 --> 09:54.240
the community together on one string of DNA. And so the next stage that's interesting

09:54.240 --> 10:00.320
in life took about another billion years. And at that stage, we have multicellular communities,

10:00.320 --> 10:04.280
communities of lots of different types of cells working together as a single organism.

10:04.560 --> 10:09.880
And in fact, we're such a multicellular community. We have lots of cells that are not out for

10:09.880 --> 10:16.360
themselves anymore. Your skin cell is really useless without a heart cell, a muscle cell,

10:16.360 --> 10:21.880
a brain cell, and so on. So these communities began to evolve so that the interesting level

10:21.880 --> 10:26.560
on which evolution was taking place was no longer a cell, but a community which we call

10:26.560 --> 10:33.560
an organism. Now the next step that happened is within these communities, these communities

10:34.040 --> 10:39.520
of cells again began to abstract information. And they began building very special structures

10:39.520 --> 10:44.600
that did nothing but process information within the community. And those are the neural structures.

10:44.600 --> 10:49.520
So neurons are the information processing apparatus of those communities of cells built

10:49.520 --> 10:55.040
up. And in fact, they began to get specialists in the community in special structures that

10:55.040 --> 11:00.960
were responsible for recording, understanding, learning information. And that was the brains

11:01.000 --> 11:06.400
and the nervous system of those communities. And that gave them an evolutionary advantage

11:06.400 --> 11:13.400
because at that point, an individual, things, learning could happen within the time span

11:14.240 --> 11:20.440
of a single organism instead of over this evolutionary time span. So an organism could

11:20.440 --> 11:24.360
for instance learn not to eat a certain kind of fruit because it tasted bad and it got

11:24.360 --> 11:29.760
sick last time it ate it. That could happen within the lifetime of a single organism whereas

11:29.800 --> 11:34.280
before they had built those special information processing structures, that would have had

11:34.280 --> 11:40.720
to be learned evolutionarily over hundreds of thousands of years by the individuals dying

11:40.720 --> 11:45.480
off today that kind of fruit. So that nervous system, the fact that they built those special

11:45.480 --> 11:50.560
information structures tremendously speeded up the whole process of evolution because

11:50.560 --> 11:56.920
evolution could now happen within an individual. It could happen in learning time scale. Then

11:56.920 --> 12:02.360
what happened was the individuals worked out, of course, tricks of communicating. And

12:02.360 --> 12:07.400
for example, the most sophisticated version that we're aware of is human language. It's

12:07.400 --> 12:12.760
really a pretty amazing invention if you think about it. Here I have a very complicated messy

12:12.760 --> 12:18.280
confused idea in my head. I'm sitting here making grunting sounds basically and hopefully

12:18.280 --> 12:23.480
constructing a similar messy confused idea in your head that bears some analogy to it.

12:23.480 --> 12:27.960
So we're taking something very complicated, turning it into sound, sequence of sounds

12:27.960 --> 12:34.960
and producing something very complicated in your brain. So this allows us now to begin

12:35.000 --> 12:42.000
to start functioning as a single organism. And so in fact what we've done is we, humanity,

12:43.800 --> 12:48.120
have started abstracting out, we're going through the same levels that multicellular

12:48.120 --> 12:53.760
organisms have gone through, abstracting out our methods of recording, presenting, processing

12:53.760 --> 13:00.760
information. So for example, the invention of language was a tiny step in that direction.

13:00.760 --> 13:07.160
Telephony, computers, videotapes, CD-ROMs, so on are all our specialized mechanisms that

13:07.160 --> 13:14.160
we've now built within our society for handling that information. And it all connects us together

13:14.360 --> 13:21.360
into something that is much bigger and much faster and able to evolve than what we were

13:22.640 --> 13:28.920
before. So now evolution can take place on the scale of microseconds. And you saw Kai's

13:28.920 --> 13:33.440
little evolutionary example where he sort of did a little bit of evolution of a convolution

13:33.440 --> 13:37.960
program right before your eyes. So now we've speeded up the timescales once again. So the

13:37.960 --> 13:42.360
first steps of the story that I told you about took a billion years of peace. And the next

13:42.400 --> 13:48.400
steps like nervous systems and brains took a few hundred million years. And the next

13:48.400 --> 13:53.760
steps like language and so on took less than a million years. And these next steps like

13:53.760 --> 13:58.480
electronics seems to be taking only a few decades. And the process is feeding on itself

13:58.480 --> 14:04.360
and becoming I guess autocatalytic is the word for it. When something reinforces its

14:04.360 --> 14:08.800
rate of change, the more it changes the faster it changes. And I think that that's what we're

14:08.840 --> 14:14.120
seeing here in this explosion of curve. We're seeing this process feeding back on itself.

14:14.120 --> 14:21.120
Now I design computers for a living. And I know the mechanisms that I use to design computers

14:22.000 --> 14:28.240
would be impossible without recent advances in computers. So right now what I do is I

14:28.240 --> 14:33.360
design objects at such complexity that it's really impossible for me to design them in

14:33.360 --> 14:37.880
the traditional sense. I don't know what every transistor in a connection machine does. There

14:37.960 --> 14:44.960
are billions of them. Instead what I do and what the designers at Thinking Machines do

14:44.960 --> 14:49.560
is we think at some level of abstraction and then we hand it to the machine and the machine

14:49.560 --> 14:54.240
takes it beyond what we could ever do. Much farther and faster than what we could ever

14:54.240 --> 15:00.560
do. And in fact sometimes it takes it by methods that we don't quite even understand. One method

15:00.560 --> 15:07.120
that's particularly interesting that I've been using a lot lately is evolution itself.

15:07.120 --> 15:13.200
So what we do is we put inside the machine a process of evolution that takes place on

15:13.200 --> 15:19.480
the microsecond time scale. So for example in the most extreme cases we can actually

15:19.480 --> 15:26.300
evolve a program by starting out with random sequences of instructions. Say computer would

15:26.300 --> 15:30.840
you please make a hundred million random sequences of instructions. Now would you please run

15:30.840 --> 15:34.960
all of those random sequences of instructions, run all of those programs and pick out the

15:34.960 --> 15:40.240
ones that came closest to do what I wanted. So in other words I define what I wanted.

15:40.240 --> 15:45.160
Let's say I want to sort numbers as a simple example I've done. So find the programs that

15:45.160 --> 15:50.040
come closest to sorting numbers. So of course random sequences of instructions are very

15:50.040 --> 15:54.800
unlikely to sort numbers. So none of them will really do it. But one of them by luck

15:54.800 --> 16:01.280
may put two numbers in the right order. And I say to the computer would you please now

16:01.320 --> 16:06.280
take the 10% of those random sequences that did the best job, save those, kill off the

16:06.280 --> 16:12.480
rest and now let's reproduce the ones that sorted numbers the best. And let's reproduce

16:12.480 --> 16:17.640
them by a process of recombination analogous to sex. Take two programs and they produce

16:17.640 --> 16:23.120
children by exchanging their subroutines and the children inherit the traits, the subroutines

16:23.120 --> 16:28.160
of the two programs. So I've got now a new generation of programs that are produced by

16:28.160 --> 16:34.040
combinations of the programs that did a little bit better job. Say please repeat that process,

16:34.040 --> 16:38.440
score them again, introduce some mutations perhaps and try that again and do that for

16:38.440 --> 16:43.720
another generation. Well every one of those generations just takes a few milliseconds.

16:43.720 --> 16:48.400
So I can do the equivalent of millions of years of evolution on that within the computer

16:48.400 --> 16:53.840
in a few minutes or in the complicated cases in a few hours. At the end of that I end up

16:53.880 --> 16:58.320
with programs that are absolutely perfect at sorting numbers. In fact they are programs

16:58.320 --> 17:02.760
that are much more efficient than programs I could have ever written by hand. Now if

17:02.760 --> 17:06.920
I look at those programs I can't tell you how they work. I've tried looking at them

17:06.920 --> 17:12.480
and tell you how they work. They're obscure, weird programs. But they do the job and in

17:12.480 --> 17:16.880
fact I know I'm very confident that they do the job because they come from a line of hundreds

17:16.880 --> 17:21.880
of thousands of programs that did the job. In fact their life depended on doing the job.

17:21.880 --> 17:28.880
I was writing in a 747 with Marvin Minsky once and he pulls out this card and he said

17:32.640 --> 17:38.960
oh look, look at this, it says this plane has hundreds of thousands of tiny parts working

17:38.960 --> 17:45.960
together to make you a safe flight. Doesn't that make you feel confident? In fact we know

17:46.600 --> 17:50.920
that the engineering process doesn't work very well when it gets complicated. So we're

17:50.960 --> 17:56.960
beginning to depend on computers to do a process that's very different than engineering. And

17:56.960 --> 18:02.640
it lets us produce things of much more complexity than normal engineering lets us produce. And

18:02.640 --> 18:06.800
yet we don't quite understand the objects of it. So in a sense it's getting ahead of

18:06.800 --> 18:11.840
us. We're now using those programs to make much faster computers so that we'll be able

18:11.840 --> 18:18.280
to run this process much faster. So it's feeding back on itself. The thing is becoming faster

18:18.280 --> 18:22.520
and that's why I think it seems so confusing because all of these technologies are feeding

18:22.520 --> 18:28.920
back on themselves. We're taking off. And what we are is we're at a point in time which

18:28.920 --> 18:33.880
is analogous to when single cell organisms were turning into multi cell organisms. So

18:33.880 --> 18:38.520
we're the amoebas and we can't quite figure out what the hell this thing is we're creating.

18:38.520 --> 18:43.060
We're right at that point of transition. But I think that there really is something coming

18:43.060 --> 18:47.540
along after us. I think it's very haughty of us to think that we're the end product

18:47.540 --> 18:54.540
of evolution. And I think all of us here are a part of producing whatever that next thing

18:54.660 --> 19:00.660
is. So lunch is coming along and I think I will stop at that point before I get selected

19:00.660 --> 19:00.940
out.

